1. INTRODUCTION
Additive manufacturing is an advanced manufacturing technology that draws the attention from the thermal engineers. In heat exchanger applications, Thompson et al. (2015) indicated that SLM technology has high potential as it can produce more compact devices while maintaining high mechanical properties and good thermal conductivity such as compact heat exchangers or even full-size heat exchangers with specially designed geometries. Shah and Sekulic (2003) presented that compact heat exchangers (CHE) are characterized by the use of micro- and minichannels, resulting in a high heat transfer rate in a reduced volume. The mini- and micro-tubes are the most basic components in the compact systems, and a full understanding of their heat transfer and pressure drop characteristics can provide useful information for the design and application of such systems. Various researchers have conducted studies to investigate whether the traditional heat transfer and pressure drop results for macro-tubes can directly apply to the micro-tubes. Discrepancies were found with some agreements and disagreements. Guo (2018) conducted a comprehensive review about heat transfer enhancement. In his review, passive techniques such as the rough surface was one of the methods for heat transfer augmentation. Celata (2004) indicated that roughness is considered to be one of the reasons for the difference in the prediction of correlation between micro-scale tube and macro-scale tube. In the middle of the last century, Nikuradse (1933) systematically summarized the effect of rough surface on flow characteristics by using a brass tube. He believed that the relative roughness in the laminar flow region was negligible when the relative roughness was less than 5%, while for the turbulent flow region, he proposed


