an empirical correlation for calculating the friction factor for turbulent flow in pipes. Under the combination of different relative roughness and pipe diameters (0.62 mm and 1.067 mm), Kandlikar et al. (2003) found that the coefficients of pressure drop and heat transfer increase as the values of relative roughness increase for the small diameter tubes. Moreover, changing the roughness value also affected the transition to turbulence. Yu et al. (1995) performed heat transfer experiments in micro-tubes using distilled water as test fluid. Their experiments found that the transition occurred for Reynolds numbers from 1700 to 6000, in agreement with the conventional theory. But the Nusselt numbers were larger than the expected theoretical values for Reynolds numbers, ranging between 6000 and 20,000. Choi et al. (1991) measured the Nusselt number and the friction factor through micro-tubes having a diameter of 3, 7, 10, 53, 81 μm with Reynolds numbers ranging between 30 and 20,000. They found that the friction factor for microtubes was lower than predicted by the Blasius (1913) equation, while the Nusselt number obtained by experiment was higher than the Nusselt number predicted by the conventional theory. Celata et al. (2002) investigated the hydraulic and single-phase heat transfer of a traditional stainless steel capillary tube with a very small internal diameter (130 μm), for Reynolds numbers in the range of 100 and 8000. Experimental results were compared to the empirical correlation. In the turbulent region, Colebrook et al. (1939) equation with the experimental mean value of the roughness overpredicts the friction factors in the entire experimental range, while the Nusselt numbers were significantly higher than that of conventional correlations. Therefore, they proposed that heat transfer and pressure drop correlations in the turbulent regime developed for macro-tubes are not applicable to micro-tubes. Stimpson et al. (2016) studied the pressure drop and heat transfer behaviors for gas flow inside 3D-printed mini rectangular channels. Special attention was paid to the surface roughness. They concluded that pressure drop and heat transfer were both increased in comparison with the smooth rectangular channel. Based on the experimental results, Stimpson et al. (2017) analyzed the roughness effect on pressure drop and heat transfer systematically and developed an empirical correlation to predict heat transfer and pressure drop. However, this correlation is only applicable to air flow according to the authors. It can be noted from the above reviews that the previous research focused on traditional tubes, while studies on 3D-printed tubes were less involved. Whether the existing research results for traditional tubes can be applied in 3D-printed tubes is still unclear. Selective Laser Melting (SLM) metal 3D-printing technology is the most commonly used and high-precision metal laser 3D-printing forming technology. Therefore, the purpose of this study is to experimentally investigate the heat transfer and pressure drop characteristics using tube printed by SLM technology and traditional cold rolled stainless steel tube in the vertical direction under isothermal and non-isothermal boundary conditions.
2. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP
A schematic diagram of the overall experimental apparatus used for hydraulic characteristics and thermal behavior experiments is shown in Fig. 1. In order to accurately measure heat transfer and pressure drop characteristics, the experimental setup consists of four parts, namely fluid circulation system, test bench and instruments, test section, and the data acquisition system. Distilled water was selected as the working fluid of the fluid circulation system. At the beginning of the experiment, the pressure valve was opened to push the test fluid out of the pressure vessel; it passed through the flow meter and test section and was finally recovered by the receiving tank. After the experiment, the working fluid in the receiving tank was re-pumped into the pressure vessel by the water pump to complete the cycle. The mass flow rate was measured by a micro-motion F series flow meter with an accuracy of ± 1.75%. Although there is a real-time monitor on the flow meter, for a more precise measurement, it was connected to the data acquisition system and the LabVIEW program was used to record the average value of the flow rate.


