3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
To validate the new experimental setup, experiments were first performed with traditional stainless steel tubes. Figure 5 shows the comparison of fully developed heat transfer data for a traditional tube (at the x/Di = 250) with the data of Ghajar and Tam (1994) for a 15.8 mm (at the
x/Di = 200) stainless steel tube. The square-edge inlet data was used as a comparison due to
its similarity to the current inlet type. The current heat transfer data follows the data trend of Ghajar and Tam (1994) in the laminar region, transition region, or turbulent region. In addition, the laminar isothermal friction factors of traditional tube compared well with the Darcy (1858) friction factor for Reynolds numbers varying from 100 and 2200 with a maximum deviation of 6.1% and an average deviation of 2.3%. In the turbulent flow region, the experimental data also compared well with the Blasius (1913) equation between Reynolds numbers of 4000 and 10,000, with a maximum deviation of 1.7% and an average deviation of 1% (see Fig. 6). As a result, the experimental setup and experimental data were confirmed to be valid. After the verification of the experimental setup, the heat transfer and pressure drop experiments for traditional tube and 3D-printed tube were conducted. Figure 7 clearly shows the heat transfer characteristics for different manufacturing techniques under uniform wall heat flux boundary condition. In the figure, it is shown that the heat transfer characteristics and magnitudes are almost the same for the traditional tube and 3D-printed tube in the laminar region. Based on 100 laminar experimental data points, the average deviation between these two tubes were 7.7%. However, for the turbulent region, from 96 experimental data points, the Nusselt number for the 3D-printed tube increased by an average of 45% in comparison to the traditional tube. This dis
