• The start of the transition region for the traditional tube and the 3D-printed tube were different. The 3D-printed tube has a smaller transition Reynolds number, and it is due to the larger relative roughness. • In the laminar region, the influence of different manufacturing technologies on the heat transfer is not obvious. However, for the transition and turbulent regions, the heat transfer and pressure drop for the 3D-printed tube increased in comparison to the traditional tube. The heat transfer enhancement for the 3D-printed tube is due to the surface characteristics of the SLM manufacturing method that causes rougher inner surface and larger relative roughness. These surface characteristics enhance fluid mixing, and therefore increase heat transfer. The same observation was made for the pressure drop results; these surface characteristics increase the friction factor significantly. • Based on the obtained experimental data, an empirical correlation was developed to predict the heat transfer coefficients and friction factors of 3D-printed tubes using the SLM manufacturing method in turbulent flow regime. In the development of the Nusselt number correlation, a total of 48 experimental data points were used. The absolute average deviation between the results predicted by the correlation and the experimental
